  """
// Particle Combinations (02b §7).
// All stable hadrons follow one rule: consume complete structure.
//
// Baryons (3 quarks):
//   Each quark on one leg of SU(2)-complex.
//   Full complex consumed: XY + YZ + ZX.
//   Three colors (RGB) = color neutral.
//   99% of mass is chirality of the unified rotating structure,
//   not individual quark masses.
//
// Mesons (quark + antiquark):
//   One leg of SU(2)-complex.
//   Color + anticolor = neutral.
//   Lighter than baryons: less chiral structure.
//
// Forbidden:
//   Diquark (2 quarks): incomplete, not color neutral (Law 1).
//   Four quarks: decomposes into baryon + meson or two mesons.
//   Single quark: unresolved SU(3) coupling (Law 3).
//
// Exotic hadrons (pentaquarks, tetraquarks) exist but are unstable:
// their combined structure exceeds native Planck shape geometry.
// Separation into native-fitting structures is favored.
//
// Glueballs: lattice fluctuations forming self-contained states.
// More stable than quark exotics but fragile as spanning structures.
//
// Derives from: theory.physics.particles, theory.physics.su3,
//               theory.coherence.law1
